# Line endings (NEWS-213).
#
# Git for Windows checks out with CRLF by default, and this repo now runs real
# work on Windows runners — bash scripts under `shell: bash`, and Node scripts
# that pattern-match multi-line file content. Both break on CRLF in ways that
# reproduce nowhere else:
#
#   - A `.sh` file with CRLF fails in Git Bash with `$'\r': command not found`,
#     or worse, silently passes a trailing `\r` into a variable.
#   - `scripts/set-version.mjs` matched `\n` between Cargo.lock lines, found
#     nothing on Windows, and exited 1 — which killed the signed Windows build of
#     a release whose tag was already public.
#
# The script is CRLF-tolerant now, but that is belt to this file's braces: the
# right fix is for these files to *be* LF everywhere, so the question stops
# coming up. Scoped to the files where it matters rather than a blanket
# `* text=auto eol=lf`, which would renormalize the whole tree in one commit.
#
# **`.ts` / `.tsx` / `.md` joined the list in NEWS-430, and the scoping is why
# they were missing.** The rule above was written when only bash and Node
# scripts ran on Windows. NEWS-419 then put the *unit suite* there, and several
# of those tests read source and prose as text:
#
#   - `sandboxable.test.ts` strips comments before scanning, with
#     `.split('\n')` and `/^\s*(?:\/\/|#|\s\*)\s.*$/`. In a JS regex `.` does not
#     match `\r`, so on a CRLF line `.*$` never reaches the end, the comment is
#     not stripped, and the guard flagged a `npx tsx` that only existed inside
#     an explanation of the rule.
#   - `npm-package.test.ts` matches fenced blocks in README.md against `\n`.
#
# Both are the same shape as the `\`-vs-`/` defect NEWS-419 fixed: a POSIX
# assumption that is invisible on the machine that writes it.

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
Cargo.toml  text eol=lf
Cargo.lock  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf

# Never touched: these are binary and a text conversion would corrupt them.
# `text=auto` would usually detect that, but the demo recording's images carry a
# `.bin` extension precisely because the app's cache does not name types.
*.bin   binary
*.png   binary
*.svgz  binary
